Description.Explanation:
Resource leaks have at least two common causes:

- Error conditions and other exceptional circumstances.

- Confusion over which part of the program is responsible for releasing the resource.

Most unreleased resource issues result in general software reliability problems. However, if an attacker can intentionally trigger a resource leak, the attacker may be able to launch a denial of service attack by depleting the resource pool.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following code executes a database query but does not release the statement nor connection resources.
<pre>
func insertUser(name:String, age:int) {
    let dbPath = URL(fileURLWithPath: Bundle.main.resourcePath ?? "").appendingPathComponent("test.sqlite").absoluteString

    var db: OpaquePointer?
    var stmt: OpaquePointer?

    if sqlite3_open(dbPath, &amp;db) != SQLITE_OK {
        print("Error opening articles database.")
        return
    }

    let queryString = "INSERT INTO users (name, age) VALUES (?,?)"

    if sqlite3_prepare(db, queryString, -1, &amp;stmt, nil) != SQLITE_OK{
        let errmsg = String(cString: sqlite3_errmsg(db)!)
        log("error preparing insert: \(errmsg)")
        return
    }

    if sqlite3_bind_text(stmt, 1, name, -1, nil) != SQLITE_OK{
        let errmsg = String(cString: sqlite3_errmsg(db)!)
        log("failure binding name: \(errmsg)")
        return
    }

    if sqlite3_bind_int(stmt, 2, age) != SQLITE_OK{
        let errmsg = String(cString: sqlite3_errmsg(db)!)
        log("failure binding name: \(errmsg)")
        return
    }

    if sqlite3_step(stmt) != SQLITE_DONE {
        let errmsg = String(cString: sqlite3_errmsg(db)!)
        log("failure inserting user: \(errmsg)")
        return
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Always release the resources to prevent memory leaks. 

<b>Example 2:</b> The following code executes a database query and releases the statement and connection resources.
<pre>
func insertUser(name:String, age:int) {
    let dbPath = URL(fileURLWithPath: Bundle.main.resourcePath ?? "").appendingPathComponent("test.sqlite").absoluteString

    var db: OpaquePointer?
    var stmt: OpaquePointer?

    if sqlite3_open(dbPath, &amp;db) != SQLITE_OK {
        print("Error opening articles database.")
        release_resources(stmt: stmt!, db: db!)
        return
    }

    let queryString = "INSERT INTO users (name, age) VALUES (?,?)"

    if sqlite3_prepare(db, queryString, -1, &amp;stmt, nil) != SQLITE_OK{
        let errmsg = String(cString: sqlite3_errmsg(db)!)
        log("error preparing insert: \(errmsg)")
        release_resources(stmt: stmt!, db: db!)
        return
    }

    if sqlite3_bind_text(stmt, 1, name, -1, nil) != SQLITE_OK{
        let errmsg = String(cString: sqlite3_errmsg(db)!)
        log("failure binding name: \(errmsg)")
        release_resources(stmt: stmt!, db: db!)
        return
    }

    if sqlite3_bind_int(stmt, 2, age) != SQLITE_OK{
        let errmsg = String(cString: sqlite3_errmsg(db)!)
        log("failure binding name: \(errmsg)")
        release_resources(stmt: stmt!, db: db!)
        return
    }

    if sqlite3_step(stmt) != SQLITE_DONE {
        let errmsg = String(cString: sqlite3_errmsg(db)!)
        log("failure inserting user: \(errmsg)")
        release_resources(stmt: stmt!, db: db!)
        return
    }
    release_resources(stmt: stmt!, db: db!)
}

func release_resources(stmt:OpaquePointer, db:OpaquePointer) {
    sqlite3_finalize(stmt)
    sqlite3_close(db)
}
</pre>

Always clear sensitive data that is no longer needed. Instead of storing sensitive data in immutable objects such as <code>String</code>s, use byte arrays or character arrays that you can clear programmatically.

According to Apple Security Guide: "Passwords must be kept in memory for the minimum amount of time possible and should be written over, not just released, when no longer needed. It is possible to read data out of memory even if the application no longer has pointers to it".

<b>Example 2:</b> The following code early finishes the <code>password</code> scope by enclosing it on a <code>do</code> block.
<pre>
    do {
        let password = passwordTextField.text!
        // use the password
    }
</pre>
